# Automation and extension points

Use structured output, explicit targets, and catalog-backed extensions rather than parsing terminal text. This guide builds on a validated application from <GuideRef slug="cargo-upwell">Cargo Upwell</GuideRef>.

## Automate validation

Write a JSON report before branching on the process status. A failed validation can still produce structured diagnostics that belong in a CI artifact.

<Example title="Keep a doctor report in CI">

```sh
mkdir -p target/upwell
```

Run `doctor` with the same explicit target selection used by your build:

```sh
if ! cargo upwell doctor \
  --package api \
  --bin api-server \
  --format json > target/upwell/doctor.json; then
  jq '.outcome, .diagnostics[] | {code, severity, message, fix}' target/upwell/doctor.json
  exit 1
fi
```

</Example>

`check --format json` and `doctor --format json` emit one versioned command report with `schema`, `command`, `outcome`, `exit_code`, target facts when available, and canonical diagnostics. Treat those fields as the machine contract.

Exit code `0` is success; `1` is validation failure; `2` is CLI misuse; `3` is target selection failure; `4` is Cargo build failure; `5` is probe failure; and `6` is an operational failure such as cancellation or a lock. Retain both the exit category and diagnostics. For a failed `export --format envelope` or graph JSON, preserve stdout before handling its nonzero exit status.

### Keep compatibility checks explicit

`doctor` compares the selected application's framework version with the installed `cargo-upwell` release. Keep that comparison in automation rather than deriving compatibility from a documentation label. Tooling documents and probe envelopes also carry a semantic tooling-schema version; consumers should validate it with a semantic-version requirement, and a renderer must declare matching renderer-ABI and tooling-schema requirements before Cargo Upwell invokes it.

## Generate a project from a known template

List the templates active for the current machine or project, choose an ID, then generate non-interactively. Passing the template explicitly keeps the workflow reproducible outside a terminal.

<Example title="Create a catalog-backed service">

```sh
cargo upwell templates --catalog .upwell/catalog.toml
```

Generate the selected template and avoid initializing version control when another tool owns it:

```sh
cargo upwell init apps/payments \
  --name payments \
  --template upwell/application \
  --define workspace=false \
  --no-vcs
```

</Example>

Built-in templates are `upwell/application`, `upwell/plugin`, and `upwell/protocol`. Use `--workspace` to register a generated project in its immediate parent Cargo workspace; the generator rolls back the directory if that registration fails. During development against an unreleased framework checkout, use `--upwell-path /path/to/upwell`.

For a one-off local template, pass `--template-path ../templates/schema-viewer`; it conflicts with `--template` and `--catalog`. The default catalog path is platform-native. On macOS it is `~/Library/Application Support/org.upwell-rs.Upwell/catalog.toml`; `UPWELL_CATALOG_PATH` or `--catalog` selects another file. An absent default leaves built-ins active, while an explicitly selected catalog must exist and validate.

## Add one renderer without duplicating the catalog reference

Catalogs can define templates, metadata-only tools, and WebAssembly renderers. Start from a small project catalog, then use the new format on the command it declares.

<Example title="Register and use an inspection renderer">

```toml
schema = "1"

[[entries]]
type = "renderer"
id = "acme/summary"
component = "renderers/summary.component.wasm"
commands = ["inspect"]
format = "summary"
media-type = "text/plain"
utf8 = true
abi = "^0.1"
tooling-schema = "^0.20"
```

Inspect with the format the catalog provides:

```sh
cargo upwell inspect --catalog .upwell/catalog.toml --format summary
```

</Example>

Catalog IDs are lowercase, namespaced identifiers. Relative component and template paths resolve from the catalog file. Catalog schema `1` has additional renderer capabilities and priority settings; use the installed command help and catalog validation instead of copying every option into a project catalog.

Cargo Upwell runs a renderer without host imports in a limited sandbox and validates its ABI, tooling-schema requirement, response claims, and configured UTF-8 requirement. If it fails, the command reports the renderer problem on stderr and falls back to its native default. Retain stderr and a native JSON export when renderer output must be lossless.

## Make local completion useful

Install the shell integration once, then refresh the private workspace cache after changing packages, binaries, or features.

<Example title="Enable Zsh completion for one workspace">

```sh
cargo upwell completions generate zsh > ~/.zfunc/_cargo-upwell
```

Refresh resource-aware values for the intended binary:

```sh
cargo upwell completions refresh \
  --package api \
  --bin api-server \
  --features metrics
```

</Example>

Bash, Elvish, Fish, PowerShell, and Zsh are supported. Source the generated registration from the shell startup configuration; for Fish, write it below `~/.config/fish/conf.d/`. Regenerate after upgrading Cargo Upwell.

Completion never runs Cargo or application code while you press Tab. Workspace-aware values come from the cache, which successful inspection commands also refresh on a best-effort basis. Use the absolute `UPWELL_COMPLETION_CACHE_DIR` path to isolate state in CI or separate development environments.
